   'repair' spends no task query on a named claim: its repairs cost reruns and one verdict each, not human work.
   Reading.  'bounds hold' checks, on every rejection of chain, loc, loc+dfs and repair: loc's cost <= sum pi_l +
   t*_N(u_L), with path calls the human's own and cost <= p_max d + t_max; every CERT is at the human's own
   slip; the first depth-first mistake is before N*_r; loc+dfs <= 2 min{loc, first + 1}.  unloc+dfs abstains
   when the run has no mistake (the human's own slip, passed by N, is all V objects to): without a location
   nothing can be repaired.  'fresh' reruns a problem after an uninformative rejection (at most 10, then
   abstains); '(at a human slip %)' is the share of those rejections caused by a human slip that this run's
   fresh verdict passed; the rest are runs that left the human's nominal tree without a mistake.

== Counterexamples (hand-built: root poses A in round 1 and B in round 2, assembles in round 3;
   leaves take 3 rounds; the only unlearned insight is in B's round 2 unless stated) ==
   CE2 (false pass in the run, V unlocated): root record proved; V names B; S_own from the root stalls (no hit); S_loc hits in B after 4 queries.
   CE3 (the human's slip passed by N): V names A (first in depth-first order); S_loc ends in CERT at A, the human's own claim after 4 queries; without the repair rule this is a stall although B holds a mistake.
   CE1 (fresh verdicts, no mistake anywhere): A's record in this run open, the root's open; mistakes in the run: 0; S_own descends into A (the human's own work) and ends without a hit; the full depth-first scan (9 rounds) finds none: an uninformative rejection.

(runtime 50 s)
